# {{BRAND}}

{{DOMAIN}}: an Amazon Associates review site on the affiliate-site kit
({{KIT_VERSION}}, vendored read-only in `vendor/affiliate-kit`). Scaffolded on
{{CREATED_ON}} by reusable-agents `install/create-affiliate-site.sh`.

Start with `CLAUDE.md`: the north star, the kit boundary, the Amazon data
rules and the gate contract.

## Quick start

```bash
npm ci
npm run typecheck && npm test
npm run build
bash tests/run-local-suite.sh        # the deploy gate: builds the image, needs docker
```

Local server against a database you own (never production):

```bash
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://... MIGRATE_ON_BOOT=1 SITE_HERMETIC=1 PORT=8080 npm start
```

## What the scaffold gives you

- The kit's routes (health, robots.txt, llms.txt, IndexNow key, click beacon,
  browser bundle) and AI-traffic logging, mounted first.
- A route table (`src/routes/table.ts`) with the home page, the guides index
  and article pages (from the kit's `editorial_articles`), about, disclosure,
  privacy, and the sitemaps.
- The deploy gate wired to a fixture Postgres on :{{FIXTURE_DB_PORT}} with the
  app under test on :{{GATE_PORT}}, a fixture article, and the kit's
  compliance specs. It first checks the vendored kit against its manifest
  and against the kit release it claims in reusable-agents, and never
  overlaps the gates in `tests/suite.env` `SUITE_HOST_LOCKS`.
- The gate config in two files: `tests/ui/pages.manifest.ts` (the page
  families; add one with every page route) and `tests/ui/gate.config.ts`
  (operator-owned: the compliance sources, the content-policy rule sets and
  every waiver).
- `deploy.env`, the rendered `Dockerfile.azure`, and `azure/` and `aws/`
  wrappers around the kit's deploy scripts.
- Config templates: `agents/implementer-scope.yaml`, `keep-the-lights-on.yaml`.

## Next steps

1. Fill in `site.json` (segment tags per placement and source, byline,
   theme), the niche safety boundary in `CLAUDE.md` and `{{POLICY_FILE}}`.
2. Add the niche: catalog migrations (`db/migrations/0001_...`), domain code
   under `src/{{NICHE}}/`, renderers and their routes, with a gate family for
   each in `tests/ui/pages.manifest.ts` and fixture rows in `db/fixtures/`.
   When product pages land, set the recall source (instead of the scaffold's
   recall waiver), the Amazon titles and the product-page specs' sources in
   `tests/ui/gate.config.ts`, and add a product page and a best-of list that
   exist in production to `deploy.env` `SMOKE_PATHS` (it lists only routes
   the site serves; the post-release smoke expects 200 from each).
3. On a content-policy site, install the kit's publish guard before the first
   article (`vendor/affiliate-kit/migrations/README.md`, "The publish guard"):
   until then nothing stops an author's INSERT from publishing as written.
4. Provision and deploy: `vendor/affiliate-kit/deploy/README.md`.
5. Agents: config-only directories under `agents/`; register only on the
   operator's go-ahead. Keep the implementer's dispatches for this site
   disabled until the framework enforces `agents/implementer-scope.yaml` on
   every path (`agents/README.md`, "Prerequisite").

## Upgrading the kit

```bash
bash /home/voidsstr/development/reusable-agents/install/sync-site-kit.sh {{SITE_DIR}}
bash vendor/affiliate-kit/deploy/render-dockerfile.sh --env deploy.env --write
npm ci && npm run typecheck && npm test && bash tests/run-local-suite.sh
```

The sync reports any dependency pin the new kit changed; align `package.json`
with `npm install <pkg>@<pin> --save-exact`. The kit README ("Upgrading a
site") has the full path.
